      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a23.12.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g23.4.3', 'g23.5.15', 'g23.8.15'],
          lemma: "φορτία βαρέα … φυλακτήρια … ἀδελφοί",
          title: "Heavy burdens, broad straps, and a community without titles",
          body: "Before the woes begin, a portrait of religion gone wrong in two moves — and both start from things that were *good*. The scribes ”sit on Moses' seat”: their office is legitimate, their teaching often sound (*do what they say*), and the failure is the gap — λέγουσιν καὶ οὐ ποιοῦσιν, *they say and do not do*. **Heavy burdens** on other shoulders, not a finger of their own: the exact inversion of 11:28-30, where the yoke-maker fits burdens *to* the carrier. And **φυλακτήρια** — tefillin, the small Scripture-boxes bound to arm and forehead in literal obedience to Deuteronomy 6:8 — *broadened*: not the practice condemned but its inflation into costume; likewise the lengthened fringes (Jesus wore fringes too — 9:20 hangs on one). Piety becomes wardrobe the moment its audience shifts, πρὸς τὸ θεαθῆναι, *toward being seen*. Then the countercommunity, three titles struck: no Rabbi, no Father, no καθηγητής (roughly ”Professor” or spiritual director) — not because teaching or fathering vanish, but because in a family where **one is your Teacher and you are all ἀδελφοί, siblings**, no honorific may harden into hierarchy. The church's subsequent enthusiasm for reverend doctors and holy fathers of every denomination suggests how heavy this saying weighs — and the section seals with the axiom this whole gospel has been drumming: exalt yourself, be humbled; humble yourself, be exalted. The greatest of you: your waiter.",
        },
      ],
