      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a23.13.1',
          kind: "text",
          refs: ['g23.13.9', 'g23.13.7'],
          lemma: "κλείετε τὴν βασιλείαν … ὑποκριταί",
          title: "The first woe — gatekeepers who lock the gate (and the missing v. 14)",
          body: "The **woe** (οὐαί) is a prophetic form, not a curse word — equal parts denunciation and lament, Isaiah's and Amos's genre for grief over what should have been. Seven of them follow (Matthew's favourite number for completeness), and the first is the root of all the rest: religion's professionals, keyholders by office, **shut the kingdom in people's faces** — not entering themselves, and blocking those already at the threshold. The tragedy of the gatekeeper is that his authority is real; that is what makes his ”no” effective. Everyone wounded by a church door — barred by rule, class, scandal-weariness, or the sheer performance of the insiders — has met this woe from the receiving side, and should know that the fiercest words in the gospels are aimed at the *barrers*, not the barred. A textual note: older Bibles carry a **verse 14** here (”you devour widows' houses and for a pretence make long prayers”) — it migrated in from Mark 12:40 and is absent from Matthew's earliest manuscripts, so our text runs 13 → 15, seams showing as usual. And one word deserves its history: **ὑποκριτής** was the ordinary Greek word for a *stage actor* — one who answers from under a mask. The chapter's whole indictment is theatrical: costumes (v. 5), best seats (v. 6), applause (v. 7), and now a performance of access to a door the performers neither use nor open.",
        },
      ],
